[22/04/1987 — 14:57] — SYSTEM FAILURE REPORT
Researcher: Dr. R. Centorose

At 14:57, the first anomaly struck.
Every workstation in Sector C dimmed, flickered, and shut down in perfect synchrony. No alarms. No warnings. The COLLON GRID froze mid‑refresh, its interface locked on a half‑rendered line of CEASAR’s old forty‑seven‑shift notation. I hadn’t seen that format used since the early trials.

We assumed a power fluctuation.
We were catastrophically wrong.

By 14:59, the blackout spread across the entire branch.
Backup generators failed to engage.
Manual overrides were unresponsive.
The lights dimmed to a low, pulsing glow — steady, deliberate, almost like a heartbeat.

At 15:02, the intercom activated on its own.
No voice.
Just a low, resonant hum that bypassed the ears and vibrated directly in the skull. Several staff staggered. One collapsed. The medical wing was already offline, leaving us helpless.

I attempted to access an auxiliary terminal.
The login screen dissolved into static, then reassembled into a single sentence:

“YOU SHOULD NOT HAVE LOOKED.”

The text shifted between fonts, languages, and handwriting styles — a clear sign of the entity’s influence. Writing manipulation. Code manipulation. Perception manipulation. All at once.

At 15:07, containment sensors registered a spatial distortion around the chamber.
The readings were impossible: negative distances, inverted mass signatures, temperature fluctuations that defied physics. The chamber walls rippled like fabric caught in a slow, heavy wind.

Security attempted a lockdown.
The doors unlocked instead.

At 15:11, the meltdown sequence initiated itself.
No authorization.
No countdown.
Just a sudden, blaring alarm and a cascade of system failures.

Environmental controls cycled through extremes — freezing, boiling, then settling into a temperature that instruments couldn’t quantify. The air felt thick, heavy, almost fluid.

The containment chamber’s reinforced glass fractured without impact.
Not shattered — fractured.
As if something had pressed against the idea of the glass rather than the material.

At 15:13, the chamber began collapsing inward.
Not crushed.
Not destroyed.
Folded.
As though the room were being rewritten, its geometry rearranged by something that did not understand or care about the rules of our world.

The entity did not move.
The room moved around it.

At 15:15, the last functioning camera captured a silhouette — shifting, expanding, contracting, flickering between forms that hurt to look at. The feed dissolved into static, then into nothing.

At 15:16, the breach was confirmed.

The creature did not escape.
Escape implies boundaries.
It simply erased the boundaries that once defined its prison.

[22/04/1987 — 16:42] — PERSONAL ADDENDUM
I am writing this from a maintenance corridor terminal that somehow remains functional.
The lights flicker in patterns that feel intentional.
Every reflective surface shows movement that isn’t mine.

But I am not alone.

A small group of us — five researchers, two technicians — managed to reach the emergency stairwell before the upper floors collapsed inward. The entity’s influence was weaker there, as if it had not yet rewritten that part of the facility.

The air vibrated with a low hum that followed us down the stairwell, fading only when we reached the surface exit.

We'd escaped through a hidden hatch in the middle of nowhere, the outside was..
Unchanged.
Indifferent.

We ran.

The facility seemed to implode, as we heard sounds of metal bending and snapping.

We reached the service road and followed it until the forest thinned and the lights of a small town appeared on the horizon. I do not know its name. I do not care. It was far enough.

We are sheltering in an abandoned shop, where we found food and clothing.
The others are resting.
I cannot.

I can still feel it.
Somewhere behind us.
Not following — observing.
